<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFrameworks>net9.0;net10.0</TargetFrameworks>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
    <Description>Bobcat + TUnit — projects ordinary [Test] methods into the Bobcat model, so an existing suite renders as specifications without changing runners.</Description>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- TUnit.Core, not TUnit: the adapter needs the event-receiver interfaces and TestContext,
         and nothing from the engine or the assertion library. -->
    <PackageReference Include="TUnit.Core" />

    <!--
      DELIBERATELY NO Microsoft.Testing.Platform reference (issue #402).

      Bobcat.Xunit now ships an ITestSessionLifetimeHandler, which is what gives a projected suite
      a run bracket per RUN REQUEST instead of per process. This package does not follow, for three
      reasons:

      1. TUnit.Core depends on NO test platform at all — checked in its nuspec: Microsoft.CSharp,
         System.Collections.Immutable, System.Text.Json, System.Threading.Tasks.Extensions. That is
         exactly what makes this package safe to ship beside a pinned platform, and it is the
         property a platform reference here would spend. Since bobcat#470 src pins Microsoft.Testing
         .Platform 2.5.1 (xunit.v3 4.0.2), which is also what TUnit.Engine 1.74.0 needs, so the old
         version collision is gone; the reason that remains is that a TUnit consumer's own
         TUnit.Engine should choose its platform, not this adapter.

      2. It does not need one. Since bobcat#470 the per-request bracket a warm resident command
         needs comes from TUnit.Core itself: BobcatScenarioAttribute is also an
         ILastTestInTestSessionEventReceiver and closes the run when the session's last scenario
         has ended (TUnitResidentSuiteTests drives two warm commands through a real TUnit host).

      3. MarkerStepRun's ProcessExit backstop is still there for a session that never raises the
         receiver, so a one-shot run is one run per process, as it always was.
    -->
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Bobcat\Bobcat.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
